ad and Copper Rule","health":false,"cat":"MR","measure":null,"unit":null,"limit":null,"from":"2003-01-01","to":null,"resolved":true,"system":"Goodrich Mobile Home Park"}],"more":0},{"year":2001,"tests":[],"violations":[{"y":2001,"contaminant":"Lead and Copper Rule","health":false,"cat":"MR","measure":null,"unit":null,"limit":null,"from":"2001-07-01","to":null,"resolved":true,"system":"Goodrich Mobile Home Park"}],"more":0},{"year":2000,"tests":[],"violations":[{"y":2000,"contaminant":"Lead and Copper Rule","health":false,"cat":"MR","measure":null,"unit":null,"limit":null,"from":"2000-10-01","to":null,"resolved":true,"system":"Evergreen Springs - System #3"},{"y":2000,"contaminant":"Lead and Copper Rule","health":false,"cat":"MR","measure":null,"unit":null,"limit":null,"from":"2000-10-01","to":null,"resolved":true,"system":"Evergreen Springs - System #4"}],"more":0}],"first_year":2000,"last_year":2025,"explain":{"Copper":{"plain":"Copper, like lead, usually comes from the plumbing rather than the source \u2014 most often from copper pipe and its fittings, especially where water sits still.","effect":"Copper is an essential nutrient, but some people who drink water containing copper in excess of the action level over a relatively short amount of time could experience gastrointestinal distress. Some people who drink water containing copper in excess of the action level over many years could suffer liver or kidney damage.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Lead and Copper Rule","who":"People with Wilson's disease should consult their doctor.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Copper"},"Gross Alpha, Excl. Radon and U":{"plain":"Gross alpha is a screening measurement rather than a single substance. It counts alpha-particle radioactivity from naturally occurring elements in rock, and a high reading prompts testing for the specific ones.","effect":"Certain minerals are radioactive and may emit a form of radiation known as alpha radiation. Some people who drink water containing alpha emitters in excess of the maximum contaminant level over many years may have an increased risk of getting cancer.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Radionuclides","who":"Long-term exposure.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Gross Alpha, Excl. Radon and U"},"Lead":{"plain":"Lead is almost never in water at its source. It gets picked up on the way to the tap \u2014 from the service line under the street, from solder in older joints, from the brass in the tap itself. That makes it a property of your building more than of your town.","effect":"Infants and children who drink water containing lead in excess of the action level could experience delays in their physical or mental development. Children could show slight deficits in attention span and learning abilities. Adults who drink this water over many years could develop kidney problems or high blood pressure.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Lead and Copper Rule","who":"Children and infants first.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Lead"},"Combined Radium (-226 and -228)":{"plain":"Radium is naturally radioactive and dissolves out of certain rock formations into groundwater. Like arsenic, it is a feature of local geology.","effect":"Some people who drink water containing radium 226 or 228 in excess of the maximum contaminant level over many years may have an increased risk of getting cancer.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Radionuclides","who":"Long-term exposure.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Combined Radium (-226 and -228)"},"Lead and Copper Rule":{"kind":"rule","name":"Lead and Copper Rule","plain":"The rule governing lead and copper monitoring and corrosion control. A violation is often procedural \u2014 a missed sampling round or a late report.","effect":null,"source":null,"who":null},"Combined Uranium":{"plain":"Uranium occurs naturally in some bedrock and dissolves into groundwater. It is a chemical concern for the kidneys as well as a radiological one.","effect":"Some people who drink water containing uranium in excess of the maximum contaminant level over many years may have an increased risk of getting cancer and kidney toxicity.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Radionuclides","who":"Long-term exposure.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Combined Uranium"},"Revised Total Coliform Rule":{"plain":"Coliforms are bacteria that are mostly harmless in themselves. They are measured as an indicator: if they are present, the system may be open to something that is not harmless.","effect":"Coliforms are bacteria that are naturally present in the environment and are used as an indicator that other, potentially harmful, waterborne pathogens may be present or that a potential pathway exists through which contamination may enter the drinking water distribution system.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Revised Total Coliform Rule","who":"A warning sign rather than a harm in itself.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Revised Total Coliform Rule"},"Nitrate":{"plain":"Nitrate usually comes from fertiliser run-off, septic systems, or manure. It is most common in farming areas and in shallow wells.","effect":"Infants below the age of six months who drink water containing nitrate in excess of the maximum contaminant level could become seriously ill and, if untreated, may die. Symptoms include shortness of breath and blue-baby syndrome.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Nitrate","who":"Infants under six months. This is the fastest-acting risk on this list.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Nitrate"}}}